# Jain Ethics and Philosophy
Tamil title: ஜைன நெறியும் தத்துவமும்

Speech delivered by Arignar Anna at the Mahavira birthday celebration on 2-4-1967:

When a European who had served in Vellore for thirty-five years retired and was preparing to return to his homeland, I too was invited to his farewell. Though he had come from thousands of miles away and was a foreigner, he had served nobly by building hospitals, schools, and charitable institutions for the benefit of others. With that thought, I went to bid farewell to that Christian, who was leaving for America.

At that time, I received a threatening letter from a Hindu in Vellore. It said, “If you come to participate in the farewell for a Christian, we shall show you black flags.” I jokingly replied then, “There is black in our party flag too.”

Though people may have come from far away and may be outsiders, the people of Tamil Nadu must welcome the good work they have done and must undertake similar work themselves. I am delighted to see members of the Jain faith establishing hospitals and colleges and using the wealth they have earned for good purposes.

People of the Jain faith live in Kanchipuram, where I was born, and in the small villages around it. I have associations with them. Some scholars of Tamil even say that this path belongs especially to Tamils. I therefore wish to state that I have a connection with and an involvement in this path.

It is in our country, more than anywhere else in the world, that great sages and guides have arisen. Sometimes I even think, “Perhaps such great people need not have appeared; perhaps the noble thoughts they gave need not have arisen.”

Despite possessing many distinguished guides, we have deteriorated to this extent. The fact that we have not attained an elevated condition is what gives rise to such a thought. Countries in which no such guides arose have advanced and developed greatly. Yet although we had guides to lead us, we did not advance. The reason is that we remained people who spoke but failed to act.

Therefore, from now on, we must demonstrate through action what we say. We repeatedly say, “The Buddha said this; the Siddhas said that; Mahavira, the Prophet Muhammad, and Gandhi said these things.” But do we put them into practice? When someone asks, “You have said that he taught this and another taught that; what are you going to do?” we see only people standing startled.

We must therefore not stop with speaking; we must demonstrate through action. Only then will we have understood those noble paths. We must follow elevated thinkers such as Mahavira.

You have preserved well the Jain path that arose thousands of years ago. You do not merely walk in that path yourselves; you also care that members of other communities should follow it, live by it, and prosper. I am bound to praise you for that.

The philosophies taught by Mahavira are good paths and noble principles. I see written here such teachings as “restrain the five senses” and “do not place excessive desire upon possessions.” If the people of the world had truly understood and realized all these truths, quarrels and conflicts would not have arisen.

All the paths that guide human society may be four-storey buildings, yet proper stairways have been built only for two or three floors. From the third floor, one faces the need to climb a hanging rope ladder to reach the next.

I have close associations and familiarity with scholars and elders knowledgeable about Jainism. We have discussed the Jain faith many times. During those discussions, I felt sorrow, not because its principles were unsound, but because we have not developed enough to accept them. Because we lack the development suited to those moral principles, I will not say that all those principles should be forgotten.

---NOTES---
The source is a complete speech record ending with the distinction between the worth of Jain principles and society’s readiness to practise them.
Religious and historical descriptions are translated in the speaker’s voice and are not independently verified here.
